<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n103747" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήβεννα</span></b> , ἡ, also <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τήβεννος</span></b> , ἡ, <i><span class="en">
			D.H.
			3.61
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			Plu.
			<u>Rom.</u>
			26
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			<u>Cor.</u>
			9
		</span></i>, etc., = Lat.<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>toga</i> (incl. its varieties the <i>trabea</i>, etc.), <i><span class="en">
				<u>Inscr.Délos</u>
				1442
			</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				<u>B</u>
				34
			</span></i>
			<span class="entity">(ii B.C.)</span>, <i><span class="en">
				Plb.
				10.4.8
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				D.H.
				2.70
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				3.61
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				5.47
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				6.13
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				D.S.
				5.40
			</span></i>; worn by Antiochus Epiphanes, <i><span class="en">
				Plb.
				26.1A.
			</span></i><i><span class="en">
				2
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ptol.Euerg.
				3
			</span></i>
			<i><span class="en">
				J.
			</span></i>: said to be derived from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Τήμενος </span>(
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Τήβεννος</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Suid.
				</span></i>
			) the Arcadian, <i><span class="en">
				Artem.
				2.3
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Poll.
				7.61
			</span></i> (where <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τηβεννίς</span></b>  is
			prob. cj. for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τημενίς</span>); but <i><span class="en">
				D.H.
				3.61
			</span></i> expressly doubts its Hellenic origin. (The form of the word,
			and the context in <i><span class="en">
				D.H.
			</span></i> l.c., suggest that it is Etruscan, but K. O.
			Müller's conjecture <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Τυρρηνοί</span> for <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τύραννοι</span>
			in <i><span class="en">
				<u>EM</u>
				756.26
			</span></i> is doubtful.)</div>
